# apps/web-next — environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored) or export the values in your shell. `next dev`,
# `next build` and `next start` all read `.env*` from this directory.

# ── required-ish (it has a working default) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Where the portal API answers **from this server's point of view**. Two consumers, one
# value:
#   - `lib/api.ts`            — the server-side article reads
#   - `next.config.ts`        — the `/api/v1/:path*` rewrite that carries the browser's
#                               comment and session calls to the API
# The default matches what `apps/api` binds in development (loopback, port 3001 —
# see its `server.ts`), so a normal local setup needs nothing here.
#
# With nothing listening on it, reads degrade to the empty state instead of throwing:
# `next build` must succeed without an API, because CI has postgres/redis/minio services
# and no api process (design D-3). To see that path on purpose:
#   API_INTERNAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1 pnpm --filter web-next build
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/v1

# ── optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This site's own public origin, used only to make `og:url` and
# `<link rel="canonical">` absolute (app/layout.tsx's `metadataBase`). Unset, Next
# emits the path as written; a crawler resolves it against the request origin, so
# nothing is wrong in dev — the tags just are not absolute.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.com

# NOTE: there is no API base URL for the browser here on purpose. Client-side calls
# go to same-origin `/api/v1` (design D-3), because `apps/api` allows credentials for
# exactly one CORS origin — `PORTAL_WEB_ORIGIN`, the SPA's `http://localhost:5175`.
# A `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` pointing straight at port 3001 would be refused by that allow list
# and "fixing" it would mean widening a server-side auth surface for a dev port.
